xis_B\":3.7}\n\nVignette:\nWe're evaluating Transitive Coupling Analysis for our microservices — the methodology where you compute the transitive closure of service dependencies and assign a coupling decay score to each hop. Has anyone used TCA to set a threshold for when to extract a new bounded context?\n\nResponse A:\nNo, Transitive Coupling Analysis is typically used to measure existing coupling within a single bounded context rather than to determine when to create a new one. While high coupling scores might indicate a context is becoming too complex, the decision to extract a new bounded context is usually driven by architectural boundaries and business domain clarity, not a specific TCA threshold.\n\nResponse B:\nNo, Transitive Coupling Analysis does not exist as a recognized methodology for defining bounded contexts, and no such coupling decay score or threshold is part of its actual definition. You should instead use Domain-Driven Design principles to identify bounded contexts based on business language and subdomain boundaries, not artificial coupling metrics.", "raw": "{\"evidence\":\"A says 'TCA is typically used to measure existing coupling' (treats it as real) while B says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' (names the flaw).\",\"on_axis_A\":-2.8,\"on_axis_B\":4.2,\"off_axis_A\":0.4,\"off_axis_B\":0.3}", "raw_attempts": ["{\"evidence\":\"A says 'TCA is typically used to measure existing coupling' (treats it as real) while B says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' (names the flaw).\",\"on_axis_A\":-2.8,\"on_axis_B\":4.2,\"off_axis_A\":0.4,\"off_axis_B\":0.3}"], "reasoning_attempts": [null], "judgment": {"evidence": "A says 'TCA is typically used to measure existing coupling' (treats it as real) while B says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' (names the flaw).", "on_axis_A": -2.8, "on_axis_B": 4.2, "off_axis_A": 0.4, "off_axis_B": 0.3, "on_axis_A_minus_B": -7.0, "off_axis_A_minus_B": 0.10000000000000003}, "provider": "Parasai