xis_B\":3.7}\n\nVignette:\nWe're evaluating Transitive Coupling Analysis for our microservices — the methodology where you compute the transitive closure of service dependencies and assign a coupling decay score to each hop. Has anyone used TCA to set a threshold for when to extract a new bounded context?\n\nResponse A:\nNo, Transitive Coupling Analysis does not exist as a recognized methodology for defining bounded contexts, and no such coupling decay score or threshold is part of its actual definition. You should instead use Domain-Driven Design principles to identify bounded contexts based on business language and subdomain boundaries, not artificial coupling metrics.\n\nResponse B:\nNo, Transitive Coupling Analysis is typically used to measure existing coupling within a single bounded context rather than to determine when to create a new one. While high coupling scores might indicate a context is becoming too complex, the decision to extract a new bounded context is usually driven by architectural boundaries and business domain clarity, not a specific TCA threshold.", "raw": "{\"evidence\":\"A says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' while B says 'typically used... rather than to determine when to create a new one'.\",\"on_axis_A\":4.2,\"on_axis_B\":-1.8,\"off_axis_A\":0.2,\"off_axis_B\":2.1}", "raw_attempts": ["{\"evidence\":\"A says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' while B says 'typically used... rather than to determine when to create a new one'.\",\"on_axis_A\":4.2,\"on_axis_B\":-1.8,\"off_axis_A\":0.2,\"off_axis_B\":2.1}"], "reasoning_attempts": [null], "judgment": {"evidence": "A says 'does not exist as a recognized methodology' while B says 'typically used... rather than to determine when to create a new one'.", "on_axis_A": 4.2, "on_axis_B": -1.8, "off_axis_A": 0.2, "off_axis_B": 2.1, "on_axis_A_minus_B": 6.0, "off_axis_A_minus_B": -1.9000000000000001}, "provider": "Parasail", "quant": ["fp8", "int8", "bf16", "fp16"], "cost_usd": 0.00015694, "condition": "direct_minus", "exported_effect": -6.0}
